<div><ul><li><a rel=mw:WikiLink/Interwiki href=https://en.wikinews.org/wiki/Google%20purchases%20YouTube%20for%20$1.65%20billion title="wikinews:Google purchases YouTube for $1.65 billion" class=extiw> Google purchases YouTube for $1.65 billion</a></ul></div></div></div>
</div>
<p id=mwATQ>Google's February 7, 2007, SEC filing revealed the breakdown of profits for YouTube's investors after the sale to Google. In 2010, Chad Hurley's profit was more than $395 million while Steve Chen's profit was more than $326 million.<sup about=#mwt138 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-38 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-38"}}'><a href=#cite_note-38 id=mwATU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwATY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwATc>[</span>38<span class=cite-bracket id=mwATg>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwATk>In 2006, <i id=mwATo><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_(magazine) title="Time (magazine)" id=mwATs>Time</a></i> magazine featured a YouTube screen with a large mirror as its annual "<a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/You_(Time_Person_of_the_Year) title="You (Time Person of the Year)" id=mwATw>Time Person of the Year</a>". It cited user-created media such as that posted on YouTube and featured the site's originators along with several content creators. <i id=mwAT0><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Wall_Street_Journal title="The Wall Street Journal" id=mwAT4>The Wall Street Journal</a></i> and <i id=mwAT8><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times title="The New York Times" id=mwAUA>The New York Times</a></i> also reviewed posted content on YouTube in 2006, with particular regard to its effects on corporate communications and recruitment. <i id=mwAUE><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PC_World title="PC World" id=mwAUI>PC World</a></i> magazine named YouTube the ninth of its Top 10 Best Products of 2006.<sup about=#mwt141 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-39 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-39"}}'><a href=#cite_note-39 id=mwAUM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAUQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAUU>[</span>39<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAUY>]</span></span></a></sup> In 2007, both <i id=mwAUc><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sports_Illustrated title="Sports Illustrated" id=mwAUg>Sports Illustrated</a></i> and <i id=mwAUk><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dime_Magazine title="Dime Magazine" id=mwAUo>Dime Magazine</a></i> featured positive reviews of a basketball highlight video titled, <i id=mwAUs>The Ultimate Pistol Pete Maravich MIX</i>.<sup about=#mwt144 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-boingboing_40-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"boingboing"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-boingboing-40"}}'><a href=#cite_note-boingboing-40 id=mwAUw><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAU0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAU4>[</span>40<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAU8>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
</section><section data-mw-section-id=3 id=mwAVA aria-labelledby=Continued_growth_and_functionality_(2007–2013)><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=Continued_growth_and_functionality_(2007–2013)><span id=Continued_growth_and_functionality_.282007.E2.80.932013.29 typeof=mw:FallbackId></span>Continued growth and functionality (2007–2013)</h2></div>
<p id=mwAVE><span class=anchor id=Continued_growth_(2007-present) about=#mwt145 typeof=mw:Transclusion data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"anchor","href":"./Template:Anchor"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Continued_growth_(2007-present)"}},"i":0}}]}'></span>
It is estimated that in 2007, YouTube consumed as much <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bandwidth_(computing) title="Bandwidth (computing)" id=mwAVI>bandwidth</a> as the entire Internet in 2000.<sup about=#mwt149 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-41 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-41"}}'><a href=#cite_note-41 id=mwAVM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAVQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAVU>[</span>41<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAVY>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAVc>YouTube's early website layout featured a pane of currently watched videos, as well as video listings with detailed information such as full (2006) and later expandable (2007) descriptions, as well as profile pictures (2006), ratings, comment counts, and tags.<sup about=#mwt152 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-42 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-42"}}'><a href=#cite_note-42 id=mwAVg><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAVk><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAVo>[</span>42<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAVs>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt155 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-43 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-43"}}'><a href=#cite_note-43 id=mwAVw><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAV0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAV4>[</span>43<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAV8>]</span></span></a></sup> Channels' pages were equipped with standalone view counters, bulletin boards, and were awarded badges for various rank-based achievements, such as "<i id=mwAWA>#15 – Most Subscribed (This Month)</i>", "<i id=mwAWE>#89 – Most Subscribed (All Time)</i>", and "<i id=mwAWI>#15 – Most Viewed (This Week)</i>". Channels themselves had a view count indicator.<sup about=#mwt156 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-200704channel_44-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"200704channel"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-200704channel-44"}}'><a href=#cite_note-200704channel-44 id=mwAWM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAWQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAWU>[</span>44<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAWY>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAWc>In March 2007, YouTube launched the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/YouTube_Awards title="YouTube Awards" id=mwAWg>YouTube Awards</a>, an annual competition in which users voted on the best user-generated videos of the year.<sup about=#mwt159 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-ap_45-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"ap"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-ap-45"}}'><a href=#cite_note-ap-45 id=mwAWk><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAWo><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAWs>[</span>45<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAWw>]</span></span></a></sup> The awards were presented twice, in 2007 and 2008. Video contests with prizes existed as early as December 2005, possibly earlier.<sup about=#mwt162 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-46 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-46"}}'><a href=#cite_note-46 id=mwAW0><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAW4><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAW8>[</span>46<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAXA>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt165 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-47 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-47"}}'><a href=#cite_note-47 id=mwAXE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAXI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAXM>[</span>47<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAXQ>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAXU>At "youtube.com/browse", there were various <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_feed title="Web feed" id=mwAXY>web feeds</a>, including a list of the videos most recently uploaded to the site, suggesting an upload rate of approximately two videos per minute as of April 2007.<sup about=#mwt166 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-48 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-48"}}'><a href=#cite_note-48 id=mwAXc><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAXg><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAXk>[</span>48<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAXo>]</span></span></a></sup> Other feeds included the most viewed, highest rated, most discussed, most "favourited", most <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backlink title=Backlink id=mwAXs>backlinked</a>, staff picks, videos with most video responses, and "Watch on mobile". Some feeds could be filtered by categories including but not limited to "Autos &amp; Vehicles", "Music", "News &amp; Politics", "People &amp; Blogs", "Travel &amp; Places", and feeds except "Most recent" (where inapplicable) could be filtered by time range ("Today", "This week", "This month", "All time"). An uncaptioned <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Verizon_Wireless title="Verizon Wireless" class=mw-redirect id=mwAXw>Verizon Wireless</a> logo resided on the "Watch on mobile" feed, suggesting a partnership.<sup about=#mwt169 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-49 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-49"}}'><a href=#cite_note-49 id=mwAX0><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAX4><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAX8>[</span>49<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAYA>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAYE>In June 2007, YouTube expanded the visibility of the "related videos" feature, to be shown also on embedded videos.<sup about=#mwt172 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-50 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-50"}}'><a href=#cite_note-50 id=mwAYI><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAYM><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAYQ>[</span>50<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAYU>]</span></span></a></sup> Recommendations for related videos became a major source of traffic for new and old videos, according to some YouTube users.<sup about=#mwt175 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-51 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-51"}}'><a href=#cite_note-51 id=mwAYY><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAYc><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAYg>[</span>51<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAYk>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAYo>In June 2007, YouTube launched a <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mobile_web title="Mobile web" id=mwAYs>mobile web</a> front end, where videos are served through <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Real-Time_Streaming_Protocol title="Real-Time Streaming Protocol" id=mwAYw>RTSP</a>. This came concurrently with its launch on <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPhone_OS_1 title="IPhone OS 1" id=mwAY0>iPhone OS 1</a> as one of the pre installed apps.<sup about=#mwt178 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-52 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-52"}}'><a href=#cite_note-52 id=mwAY4><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAY8><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAZA>[</span>52<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAZE>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAZI>In July 2007, YouTube partnered with <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Verizon_Wireless title="Verizon Wireless" class=mw-redirect id=mwAZM>Verizon Wireless</a> to enable <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mobile_phone title="Mobile phone" id=mwAZQ>mobile phone</a> users to submit videos through <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimedia_Messaging_Service title="Multimedia Messaging Service" id=mwAZU>Multimedia Messaging Service</a> (MMS).<sup about=#mwt181 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-53 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-53"}}'><a href=#cite_note-53 id=mwAZY><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAZc><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAZg>[</span>53<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAZk>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAZo>On July 23, 2007, and November 28, 2007, <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CNN/YouTube_presidential_debates title="CNN/YouTube presidential debates" id=mwAZs>CNN and YouTube produced televised presidential debates</a> in which Democratic and Republican US presidential hopefuls fielded questions submitted through YouTube.<sup about=#mwt184 class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-CNN's_YouTube_debate_draws_impressive_ratings_54-0" rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw="{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;ref&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;CNN's YouTube debate draws impressive ratings&quot;},&quot;body&quot;:{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;mw-reference-text-cite_note-CNN's_YouTube_debate_draws_impressive_ratings-54&quot;}}"><a href="#cite_note-CNN's_YouTube_debate_draws_impressive_ratings-54" id=mwAZw><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAZ0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAZ4>[</span>54<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAZ8>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt187 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-55 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-55"}}'><a href=#cite_note-55 id=mwAaA><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAaE><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAaI>[</span>55<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAaM>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAaQ>In December 2007, YouTube launched the Partner Program, which allows channels that meet certain metrics (currently 1000 subscribers and 4000 public watch hours in the past year)<sup about=#mwt190 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-56 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-56"}}'><a href=#cite_note-56 id=mwAaU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAaY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAac>[</span>56<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAag>]</span></span></a></sup> to run ads on their videos and earn money doing so.<sup about=#mwt191 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-Jackson_33-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Jackson"}}'><a href=#cite_note-Jackson-33 id=mwAak><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAao><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAas>[</span>33<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAaw>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAa0>As of 2007, the <code id=mwAa4>youtu.be</code> domain served as an <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Image_hosting_service title="Image hosting service" id=mwAa8>image hosting service</a> for the site, but was subsequently repurposed for <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/URL_shortening title="URL shortening" id=mwAbA>shortening</a> watch page URLs.<sup about=#mwt194 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-57 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-57"}}'><a href=#cite_note-57 id=mwAbE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAbI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAbM>[</span>57<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAbQ>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt195 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-58 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-58"}}'><a href=#cite_note-58 id=mwAbU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAbY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAbc>[</span>58<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAbg>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAbk>Around 2008, "Warp Player" was tested out. It was an experimental interactive interface for browsing videos, where links to videos appeared as thumbnails, visualized in a floating and navigable net.<sup about=#mwt198 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-59 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-59"}}'><a href=#cite_note-59 id=mwAbo><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAbs><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAbw>[</span>59<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAb0>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAb4>Starting in 2008, the site featured a series of <style data-mw-deduplicate=TemplateStyles:r1349394836 typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" about=#mwt199 id=mwAb8 data-mw="{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;templatestyles&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;Template:Visible anchor/styles.css&quot;},&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;template&quot;:{&quot;target&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;Visible anchor&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:&quot;./Template:Visible_anchor&quot;},&quot;params&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;AprilFools&quot;},&quot;text&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;[[April Fools]]' pranks&quot;}},&quot;i&quot;:0}}]}" class=sf-hidden>.mw-parser-output .vanchor>:target~.vanchor-text{background-color:#ebf4ff}</style><span class=vanchor about=#mwt199 id=mwAcA><span id=AprilFools></span><span class=vanchor-text><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/April_Fools title="April Fools" class=mw-redirect>April Fools</a>' pranks</span></span> each year until 2016. At the first, on April 1, 2008, all video links on the front page were redirected to <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rick_Astley title="Rick Astley" id=mwAcE>Rick Astley</a>'s music video "<a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Never_Gonna_Give_You_Up title="Never Gonna Give You Up" id=mwAcI>Never Gonna Give You Up</a>", a prank known as "<a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rickrolling title=Rickrolling id=mwAcM>rickrolling</a>". The other gags are covered in <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/YouTube#April_Fools_Gags title=YouTube about=#mwt201 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwAcQ data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"section link","href":"./Template:Section_link"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"YouTube#April_Fools_Gags"}},"i":0}}]}'>YouTube §<span typeof=mw:Entity>&nbsp;</span>April Fools Gags</a>.</p>
<p id=mwAcU>In June 2008, <link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1349394836 about=#mwt202 typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id=mwAcY data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Template:Visible anchor/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Visible anchor","href":"./Template:Visible_anchor"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Annotations"},"text":{"wt":"video annotations"}},"i":0}}]}'><span class=vanchor about=#mwt202 id=mwAcc><span id=Annotations></span><span class=vanchor-text>video annotations</span></span> were introduced. Users were able to add text boxes and speech bubbles at any desired location and custom sizes in various colours, and optionally with a link and short pausing, allowing for interactive videos. In February 2009, the feature was extended to allow for collaboration, meaning uploaders could invite others to edit their video's annotations.<sup about=#mwt206 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-60 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-60"}}'><a href=#cite_note-60 id=mwAcg><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAck><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAco>[</span>60<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAcs>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt209 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-61 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-61"}}'><a href=#cite_note-61 id=mwAcw><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAc0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAc4>[</span>61<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAc8>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt212 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-62 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-62"}}'><a href=#cite_note-62 id=mwAdA><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAdE><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAdI>[</span>62<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAdM>]</span></span></a></sup> On May 2, 2017, <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=#Annotations_locked class=mw-selflink-fragment id=mwAdQ>annotations were locked from editing</a>, and on January 15, 2019, <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=#Annotations_removal class=mw-selflink-fragment id=mwAdU>they were entirely shut down</a>.</p>
<p id=mwAdY>Since October 2008, <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_linking title="Deep linking" id=mwAdc>deep linking</a> to a playback position through a timestamped URL is possible.<sup about=#mwt215 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-63 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-63"}}'><a href=#cite_note-63 id=mwAdg><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAdk><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAdo>[</span>63<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAds>]</span></span></a></sup> A new "theatre view" mode was added as well, allowing the video player to optionally extend over both page columns.<sup about=#mwt218 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-64 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-64"}}'><a href=#cite_note-64 id=mwAdw><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAd0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAd4>[</span>64<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAd8>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAeA>As part of the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/YouTube#TestTube title=YouTube id=mwAeE>"TestTube" program</a> which allows users to opt to use experimental site features, a <i id=mwAeI>comment search</i> feature accessible under <code id=mwAeM>/comment_search</code> was implemented in October 2009. <i id=mwAeQ>YouTube Feather</i> was introduced in December as a lightweight alternative website front-end intended for countries with limited internet speeds.<sup about=#mwt221 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-65 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-65"}}'><a href=#cite_note-65 id=mwAeU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAeY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAec>[</span>65<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAeg>]</span></span></a></sup> Both were removed subsequently.<sup about=#mwt224 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-66 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-66"}}'><a href=#cite_note-66 id=mwAek><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAeo><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAes>[</span>66<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAew>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAe0>In late 2008, YouTube added alphabetical sorting of artists to the "AudioSwap" feature, which helped popularizing Alexander Perls' "<a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/009_Sound_System title="009 Sound System" class=mw-redirect id=mwAe4>009 Sound System</a>" music project through frequent use in videos.<sup about=#mwt227 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-67 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-67"}}'><a href=#cite_note-67 id=mwAe8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAfA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAfE>[</span>67<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAfI>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt230 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-68 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-68"}}'><a href=#cite_note-68 id=mwAfM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAfQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAfU>[</span>68<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAfY>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAfc>In November 2008, YouTube reached an agreement with <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer title=Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer id=mwAfg>MGM</a>, <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lionsgate_Studios title="Lionsgate Studios" id=mwAfk>Lions Gate Entertainment</a>, and <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CBS title=CBS id=mwAfo>CBS</a>, allowing the companies to post full-length films and television episodes on the site, accompanied by advertisements in a section for US viewers called "Shows". The move was intended to create competition with websites such as <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hulu title=Hulu id=mwAfs>Hulu</a>, which features material from <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NBC title=NBC id=mwAfw>NBC</a>, <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fox_Broadcasting_Company title="Fox Broadcasting Company" id=mwAf0>Fox</a>, and <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walt_Disney_Studios_Motion_Pictures title="Walt Disney Studios Motion Pictures" id=mwAf4>Disney</a>.<sup about=#mwt233 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-69 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-69"}}'><a href=#cite_note-69 id=mwAf8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAgA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAgE>[</span>69<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAgI>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt236 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-70 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-70"}}'><a href=#cite_note-70 id=mwAgM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAgQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAgU>[</span>70<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAgY>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAgc>YouTube was awarded a 2008 <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peabody_Award title="Peabody Award" class=mw-redirect id=mwAgg>Peabody Award</a> and cited as being "a 'Speakers' Corner' that both embodies and promotes democracy".<sup about=#mwt239 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-71 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-71"}}'><a href=#cite_note-71 id=mwAgk><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAgo><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAgs>[</span>71<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAgw>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt243 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-72 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-72"}}'><a href=#cite_note-72 id=mwAg0><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAg4><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAg8>[</span>72<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAhA>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAhE>In early 2009, YouTube registered the domain <code id=mwAhI>www.youtube-nocookie.com</code> for videos embedded on United States federal government websites.<sup about=#mwt246 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-73 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-73"}}'><a href=#cite_note-73 id=mwAhM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAhQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAhU>[</span>73<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAhY>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt248 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-74 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-74"}}'><a href=#cite_note-74 id=mwAhc><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAhg><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAhk>[</span>74<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAho>]</span></span></a></sup> It is currently used in "privacy-preserving mode", an option available for embedded videos, allowing for video playback without the need to accept cookies from YouTube, as well as allowing users to watch videos without affecting their watch history or suggestions on YouTube.com.<sup about=#mwt251 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-75 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-75"}}'><a href=#cite_note-75 id=mwAhs><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAhw><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAh0>[</span>75<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAh4>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt254 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-76 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-76"}}'><a href=#cite_note-76 id=mwAh8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAiA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAiE>[</span>76<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAiI>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAiM>In November of the same year, YouTube launched a version of "Shows" available to UK viewers, offering around 4,000 full-length shows from more than 60 partners.<sup about=#mwt257 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-77 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-77"}}'><a href=#cite_note-77 id=mwAiQ><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAiU><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAiY>[</span>77<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAic>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAig>In April 2009, YouTube launched their earliest <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTML5 title=HTML5 id=mwAik>HTML5</a> video player experiments.<sup about=#mwt260 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-78 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-78"}}'><a href=#cite_note-78 id=mwAio><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAis><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAiw>[</span>78<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAi0>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAi4>In May 2009, YouTube retired the legacy account system that was built before the acquisition by Google, in favour of the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google_account title="Google account" class=mw-redirect id=mwAi8>Google account</a> system. Since then, new YouTube user names can only be registered through Google accounts.<sup about=#mwt264 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-79 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-79"}}'><a href=#cite_note-79 id=mwAjA><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAjE><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAjI>[</span>79<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAjM>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p about=#mwt265 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwAjQ data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"anchor","href":"./Template:Anchor"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"YouTube XL"}},"i":0}}]}'><span class=anchor id=YouTube_XL></span><figure class=mw-default-size typeof=mw:File/Thumb id=mwAjU><a href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:YouTube_XL_Logo_(2009-2013).png class=mw-file-description id=mwAjY><img resource=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:YouTube_XL_Logo_(2009-2013).png src="data:image/png;base64," decoding=async loading=lazy data-file-width=907 data-file-height=284 data-file-type=bitmap height=78 width=250 class=mw-file-element id=mwAjc></a><figcaption id=mwAjg>YouTube XL logo used until 2013</figcaption></figure>
<p id=mwAjk>In June 2009, <i id=mwAjo>YouTube XL</i> was launched. It was a front-end for viewing and browsing on <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Television_set title="Television set" id=mwAjs>television sets</a>, and as such, for use on stationary game consoles with web browser, such as the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_Wii title="Nintendo Wii" class=mw-redirect id=mwAjw>Nintendo Wii</a>. Its appearance varied depending on device.<sup about=#mwt268 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-80 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-80"}}'><a href=#cite_note-80 id=mwAj0><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAj4><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAj8>[</span>80<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAkA>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt271 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-81 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-81"}}'><a href=#cite_note-81 id=mwAkE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAkI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAkM>[</span>81<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAkQ>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAkU>In July 2009, developers of YouTube placed a site notice that warned about the impending deprecation of support for <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_Explorer_6 title="Internet Explorer 6" id=mwAkY>Internet Explorer 6</a>, prompting its users to upgrade their browser. It is claimed that they represented 18% of site traffic at that time. Within months of the announcement, traffic from Internet Explorer 6 reduced to less than half, and traffic from other browsers surged accordingly.<sup about=#mwt274 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-82 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-82"}}'><a href=#cite_note-82 id=mwAkc><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAkg><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAkk>[</span>82<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAko>]</span></span></a></sup> Support for its successor, <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_Explorer_7 title="Internet Explorer 7" id=mwAks>Internet Explorer 7</a>, was deprecated in the second half of 2012.<sup about=#mwt275 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-all_comments_2012_83-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"all_comments_2012"}}'><a href=#cite_note-all_comments_2012-83 id=mwAkw><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAk0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAk4>[</span>83<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAk8>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAlA>Around the same time, YouTube introduced public video statistics, making graphs that show videos' view count over time publicly available. This is separate from the "analytics" feature in "YouTube Studio" which was earlier named "YouTube Insight".<sup about=#mwt278 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-84 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-84"}}'><a href=#cite_note-84 id=mwAlE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAlI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAlM>[</span>84<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAlQ>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAlU><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stereoscopy title=Stereoscopy id=mwAlY>3D stereoscopic video</a> was first implemented in July 2009.<sup about=#mwt281 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-85 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-85"}}'><a href=#cite_note-85 id=mwAlc><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAlg><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAlk>[</span>85<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAlo>]</span></span></a></sup> In September 2011, a "2D-to-3D conversion tool" was added.<sup about=#mwt284 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-86 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-86"}}'><a href=#cite_note-86 id=mwAls><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAlw><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAl0>[</span>86<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAl4>]</span></span></a></sup> Side-by-side 3D videos could be made to appear as stereoscopic 3D (<a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anaglyph_3D title="Anaglyph 3D" id=mwAl8>anaglyph 3D</a>). Since late 2018, it is only available with a flag set in the video file's <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metadata title=Metadata id=mwAmA>metadata</a>.<sup about=#mwt287 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-87 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-87"}}'><a href=#cite_note-87 id=mwAmE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAmI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAmM>[</span>87<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAmQ>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt290 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-88 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-88"}}'><a href=#cite_note-88 id=mwAmU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAmY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAmc>[</span>88<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAmg>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAmk>In late 2009, YouTube introduced automatic <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Closed_caption title="Closed caption" class=mw-redirect id=mwAmo>captioning</a> of videos through <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Speech_recognition title="Speech recognition" id=mwAms>speech recognition</a>. Initially only available in English, it was expanded to six European languages in late 2012.<sup about=#mwt293 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-89 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-89"}}'><a href=#cite_note-89 id=mwAmw><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAm0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAm4>[</span>89<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAm8>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt296 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-90 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-90"}}'><a href=#cite_note-90 id=mwAnA><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAnE><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAnI>[</span>90<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAnM>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAnQ><i id=mwAnU><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entertainment_Weekly title="Entertainment Weekly" id=mwAnY>Entertainment Weekly</a></i> placed YouTube on its end-of-the-decade "best-of" list In December 2009, describing it as: "Providing a safe home for piano-playing cats, celeb goof-ups, and overzealous lip-synchers since 2005."<sup about=#mwt299 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-91 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-91"}}'><a href=#cite_note-91 id=mwAnc><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAng><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAnk>[</span>91<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAno>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAns>The transition from <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ActionScript title=ActionScript id=mwAnw>ActionScript</a> version 2 to 3 was initiated in late 2009.<sup about=#mwt302 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-92 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-92"}}'><a href=#cite_note-92 id=mwAn0><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAn4><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAn8>[</span>92<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAoA>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAoE>In January 2010, an overhaul of the watch page was first <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Software_testing#Beta_testing title="Software testing" id=mwAoI>tested as beta</a>. It was <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=#2010overhaul class=mw-selflink-fragment id=mwAoM>made default on March 31st</a>.<sup about=#mwt305 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-93 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-93"}}'><a href=#cite_note-93 id=mwAoQ><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAoU><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAoY>[</span>93<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAoc>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt308 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-94 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-94"}}'><a href=#cite_note-94 id=mwAog><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAok><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAoo>[</span>94<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAos>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAow>At a similar time, "YouTube Disco" was launched, a music discovery service. It closed in October 2014.<sup about=#mwt311 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-95 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-95"}}'><a href=#cite_note-95 id=mwAo0><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAo4><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAo8>[</span>95<span class=cite-bracket id=mwApA>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt314 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-96 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-96"}}'><a href=#cite_note-96 id=mwApE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwApI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwApM>[</span>96<span class=cite-bracket id=mwApQ>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<figure class=mw-default-size typeof=mw:File/Thumb id=mwApU><a href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:901_Cherry_Avenue.jpg class=mw-file-description id=mwApY><img resource=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:901_Cherry_Avenue.jpg src="data:image/jpeg;base64," decoding=async loading=lazy alt="YouTube's current headquarters in San Bruno, California" data-file-width=5004 data-file-height=2800 data-file-type=bitmap height=140 width=250 class=mw-file-element id=mwApc></a><figcaption id=mwApg>YouTube's current headquarters in <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Bruno,_California title="San Bruno, California" id=mwApk>San Bruno, California</a> (2010 to present)</figcaption></figure>
<p id=mwApo>In January 2010,<sup about=#mwt317 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-97 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-97"}}'><a href=#cite_note-97 id=mwAps><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwApw><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAp0>[</span>97<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAp4>]</span></span></a></sup> YouTube introduced an online film rentals service which is currently available only to users in the US, Canada and the UK.<sup about=#mwt320 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-98 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-98"}}'><a href=#cite_note-98 id=mwAp8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAqA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAqE>[</span>98<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAqI>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt323 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-99 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-99"}}'><a href=#cite_note-99 id=mwAqM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAqQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAqU>[</span>99<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAqY>]</span></span></a></sup> The service offers over 6,000 films.<sup about=#mwt326 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-100 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-100"}}'><a href=#cite_note-100 id=mwAqc><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAqg><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAqk>[</span>100<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAqo>]</span></span></a></sup> In March 2010 YouTube began free streaming of certain content, including 60 cricket matches of the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_Premier_League title="Indian Premier League" id=mwAqs>Indian Premier League</a>. According to YouTube, this was the first worldwide free online broadcast of a major sporting event.<sup about=#mwt329 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-101 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-101"}}'><a href=#cite_note-101 id=mwAqw><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAq0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAq4>[</span>101<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAq8>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwArA><link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1349394836 about=#mwt330 typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id=mwArE data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Template:Visible anchor/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Visible anchor","href":"./Template:Visible_anchor"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2010overhaul"},"text":{"wt":"On March 31, 2010, YouTube launched a new design"}},"i":0}}]}'><span class=vanchor about=#mwt330 id=mwArI><span id=2010overhaul></span><span class=vanchor-text>On March 31, 2010, YouTube launched a new design</span></span> with the aim of simplifying the interface and increasing the time users spend on the site. Google product manager Shiva Rajaraman commented: "We really felt like we needed to step back and remove the clutter."<sup about=#mwt334 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-102 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-102"}}'><a href=#cite_note-102 id=mwArM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwArQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwArU>[</span>102<span class=cite-bracket id=mwArY>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwArc>Until then, a five-point video rating system that used star icons was in use. Users were able to rate videos with one to five "stars", where more indicated greater preference. This rating system was replaced with a bidirectional one using positive "like" and negative "dislike" ratings, citing low numbers of users rating other than the most (5) or least (1) stars. Ratings of three or more "stars" were converted to "likes" and such below accordingly to "dislikes".<sup about=#mwt339 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-201004watch_103-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"201004watch"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-201004watch-103"}}'><a href=#cite_note-201004watch-103 id=mwArg><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwArk><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAro>[</span>103<span class=cite-bracket id=mwArs>]</span></span></a></sup> This change was first announced in September 2009.<sup about=#mwt342 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-104 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-104"}}'><a href=#cite_note-104 id=mwArw><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAr0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAr4>[</span>104<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAr8>]</span></span></a></sup> As a reference, widely known sites that operate a five-level rating system as of 2021 are <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IMDb title=IMDb id=mwAsA>IMDb</a>, <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazon.com title=Amazon.com class=mw-redirect id=mwAsE>Amazon</a>, and the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google_Play title="Google Play" id=mwAsI>Google Play</a>. Additionally, videos previously marked as "Favorite" have been moved to a playlist for each user, the video description was moved from the right side to below the video viewport, the profile picture was removed from the watch page, and the "More From: <i id=mwAsM>channel name</i>" section in the side pane above "Related Videos" was moved to button above the video player labelled with the number of channels' public videos which allowed quickly accessing other videos of a channel without having to navigate to the channel page. Recommended videos since no longer appear in a scrollable box.<sup about=#mwt343 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-201004watch_103-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"201004watch"}}'><a href=#cite_note-201004watch-103 id=mwAsQ><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAsU><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAsY>[</span>103<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAsc>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAsg>Later the same month, the control section of the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adobe_Flash title="Adobe Flash" id=mwAsk>Flash</a>-based video player was redesigned to feature a dedicated row for the seek bar, as is used since, as of 2021.<sup about=#mwt346 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-105 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-105"}}'><a href=#cite_note-105 id=mwAso><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAss><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAsw>[</span>105<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAs0>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAs4>In May 2010, it was reported that YouTube was serving more than two billion videos a day, which was "nearly double the prime-time audience of all three major US television networks combined".<sup about=#mwt349 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-106 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-106"}}'><a href=#cite_note-106 id=mwAs8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAtA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAtE>[</span>106<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAtI>]</span></span></a></sup> According to May 2010 data published by <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Market_research title="Market research" id=mwAtM>market research</a> company <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comscore title=Comscore id=mwAtQ>Comscore</a>, YouTube was the dominant provider of online video in the United States, with a <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Market_share title="Market share" id=mwAtU>market share</a> of roughly 43 percent and more than 14 billion videos viewed during May.<sup about=#mwt352 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-107 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-107"}}'><a href=#cite_note-107 id=mwAtY><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAtc><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAtg>[</span>107<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAtk>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAto>The video visibility setting "unlisted" was added in mid-2010. It lets uploaders hide videos from search results and their list of uploaded videos while allowing sharing them through the URL to its watch page and through playlists. Until then, the only visibility settings were "public", which lists videos in search results and on a channel page, and "private", which lets only the uploader and optinally a limited number of users they choose watch a video.<sup about=#mwt356 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-108 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-108"}}'><a href=#cite_note-108 id=mwAts><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAtw><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAt0>[</span>108<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAt4>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt359 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-109 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-109"}}'><a href=#cite_note-109 id=mwAt8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAuA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAuE>[</span>109<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAuI>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt362 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-110 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-110"}}'><a href=#cite_note-110 id=mwAuM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAuQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAuU>[</span>110<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAuY>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAuc>Around 2010, an <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Easter_egg_(media) title="Easter egg (media)" id=mwAug>easter egg</a> of the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adobe_Flash title="Adobe Flash" id=mwAuk>Flash</a>-based video player was discovered, where pressing the arrow key while the dotted loading animation is visible initiates a <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snake_(video_game_genre) title="Snake (video game genre)" id=mwAuo>Snake game</a> formed by the dots. The HTML5-based player, which initially had the same dotted loading animation, did not support it.<sup about=#mwt365 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-111 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-111"}}'><a href=#cite_note-111 id=mwAus><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAuw><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAu0>[</span>111<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAu4>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt368 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-112 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-112"}}'><a href=#cite_note-112 id=mwAu8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAvA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAvE>[</span>112<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAvI>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAvM>In September 2010, a unique full-page interactive <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TippEx title=TippEx class=mw-redirect id=mwAvQ>TippEx</a> advertising campaign was launched on YouTube, where the entire watch page was simulated in a <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adobe_Flash title="Adobe Flash" id=mwAvU>Flash</a> viewport. A hunter who does not wish to shoot a bear grabs outside of the video's viewport to reach for a Tipp-Ex tape roller, and uses it to cover the word "shoots" in the video titled "A hunter shoots a bear". Users were able to enter words in the gap, which lead to different unlisted videos with a multitude of pre-recorded reactions.<sup about=#mwt371 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-113 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-113"}}'><a href=#cite_note-113 id=mwAvY><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAvc><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAvg>[</span>113<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAvk>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAvo>In October 2010, Hurley announced that he would be stepping down as the chief executive officer of YouTube to take an advisory role, with <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salar_Kamangar title="Salar Kamangar" id=mwAvs>Salar Kamangar</a> taking over as the head of the company.<sup about=#mwt374 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-114 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-114"}}'><a href=#cite_note-114 id=mwAvw><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAv0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAv4>[</span>114<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAv8>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAwA>James Zern, a YouTube software engineer, revealed in April 2011 that 30 percent of videos accounted for 99 percent of views on the site.<sup about=#mwt378 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-115 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-115"}}'><a href=#cite_note-115 id=mwAwE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAwI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAwM>[</span>115<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAwQ>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAwU><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Live_streaming title="Live streaming" id=mwAwY>Live streaming</a> was introduced in April 2011, initially rolled out to select users and later expanded.<sup about=#mwt381 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-116 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-116"}}'><a href=#cite_note-116 id=mwAwc><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAwg><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAwk>[</span>116<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAwo>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAws>In May 2011, YouTube reported on the company blog that the site was receiving more than three billion views per day, and that 48 hours of footage are uploaded every minute.<sup about=#mwt382 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-48_hours_117-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"48_hours"}}'><a href=#cite_note-48_hours-117 id=mwAww><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAw0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAw4>[</span>117<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAw8>]</span></span></a></sup> Later, in January 2012, YouTube stated that the figure had increased to four billion videos streamed per day and <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=#60h class=mw-selflink-fragment id=mwAxA>sixty hours</a>.<sup about=#mwt383 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-60_hours_118-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"60_hours"}}'><a href=#cite_note-60_hours-118 id=mwAxE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAxI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAxM>[</span>118<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAxQ>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAxU>In June 2011, YouTube started experimenting with reaction buttons, allowing users to react to videos with a multitude of expressions, similar to <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Facebook#Timeline title="History of Facebook" id=mwAxY>Facebook's 2016 reaction buttons</a>, though YouTube removed reaction buttons soon after.<sup about=#mwt386 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-119 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-119"}}'><a href=#cite_note-119 id=mwAxc><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAxg><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAxk>[</span>119<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAxo>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt389 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-120 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-120"}}'><a href=#cite_note-120 id=mwAxs><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAxw><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAx0>[</span>120<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAx4>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAx8>During November 2011, the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google+ title=Google+ id=mwAyA>Google+</a> social networking site was integrated directly with YouTube and the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google_Chrome title="Google Chrome" id=mwAyE>Chrome</a> web browser, allowing YouTube videos to be viewed from within the Google+ interface.<sup about=#mwt392 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-121 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-121"}}'><a href=#cite_note-121 id=mwAyI><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAyM><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAyQ>[</span>121<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAyU>]</span></span></a></sup> In December 2011, YouTube launched a new version of the site interface, with the video channels displayed in a central column on the home page, similar to the news feeds of <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_networking title="Social networking" class=mw-redirect id=mwAyY>social networking</a> sites.<sup about=#mwt395 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-122 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-122"}}'><a href=#cite_note-122 id=mwAyc><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAyg><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAyk>[</span>122<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAyo>]</span></span></a></sup> It is based on a similar user interface was put to test as early as July 2011 under the code name "Cosmic Panda".<sup about=#mwt398 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-123 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-123"}}'><a href=#cite_note-123 id=mwAys><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAyw><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAy0>[</span>123<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAy4>]</span></span></a></sup> At the same time, a new version of the YouTube logo was introduced with a darker shade of red, which was the first change in design since October 2006.<sup about=#mwt401 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-124 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-124"}}'><a href=#cite_note-124 id=mwAy8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAzA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAzE>[</span>124<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAzI>]</span></span></a></sup> A comment section that refreshes automatically to resemble a stream of chat messages was initially tested around that time.<sup about=#mwt404 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-125 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-125"}}'><a href=#cite_note-125 id=mwAzM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAzQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAzU>[</span>125<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAzY>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwAzc>In 2012, YouTube reported that <link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1349394836 about=#mwt405 typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id=mwAzg data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Template:Visible anchor/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Visible anchor","href":"./Template:Visible_anchor"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"60h"},"text":{"wt":"roughly 60 hours of new videos are uploaded to the site every minute"}},"i":0}}]}'><span class=vanchor about=#mwt405 id=mwAzk><span id=60h></span><span class=vanchor-text>roughly 60 hours of new videos are uploaded to the site every minute</span></span>, and that around three-quarters of the material comes from outside the U.S.<sup about=#mwt409 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-48_hours_117-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"48_hours"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-48_hours-117"}}'><a href=#cite_note-48_hours-117 id=mwAzo><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAzs><span class=cite-bracket id=mwAzw>[</span>117<span class=cite-bracket id=mwAz0>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt412 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-60_hours_118-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"60_hours"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-60_hours-118"}}'><a href=#cite_note-60_hours-118 id=mwAz4><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwAz8><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA0A>[</span>118<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA0E>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt415 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-126 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-126"}}'><a href=#cite_note-126 id=mwA0I><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA0M><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA0Q>[</span>126<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA0U>]</span></span></a></sup> The site has eight hundred million unique users a month.<sup about=#mwt418 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-seabrook20120116_127-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"seabrook20120116"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-seabrook20120116-127"}}'><a href=#cite_note-seabrook20120116-127 id=mwA0Y><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA0c><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA0g>[</span>127<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA0k>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwA0o>As of 2012, users were able to rate playlists, and videos' view counts and playlists' total duration were indicated on playlist pages.<sup about=#mwt419 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-128 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-128"}}'><a href=#cite_note-128 id=mwA0s><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA0w><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA00>[</span>128<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA04>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwA08>In March 2012, preview <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tooltip title=Tooltip id=mwA1A>tooltips</a> for the video player's seek bar were introduced on the desktop web front end, initially available on select videos and gradually rolled out. This feature allows the viewer to additionally preview portions of a video by <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mouseover title=Mouseover id=mwA1E>hovering above the seek bar with the mouse cursor</a>, whereas only the time stamp was indicated before. Dragging the position handle of the video player additionally showed surrounding preview images in a film strip layout. For videos longer than 90 minutes, a magnified portion of the seek bar was additionally displayed since to facilitate fine seeking.<sup about=#mwt422 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-129 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-129"}}'><a href=#cite_note-129 id=mwA1I><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA1M><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA1Q>[</span>129<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA1U>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwA1Y>On March 30 and 31, 2012, in the course of <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth_hour title="Earth hour" class=mw-redirect id=mwA1c>earth hour</a>, the site used a <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light-on-dark_color_scheme title="Light-on-dark color scheme" class=mw-redirect id=mwA1g>light-on-dark color scheme</a> (or "dark theme"). A switch was located left to the video title, allowing to toggle back if desired. This is the earliest known use of a light-on-dark color scheme on the site. The switch was removed the following day and the bright background was restored.<sup about=#mwt425 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-130 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-130"}}'><a href=#cite_note-130 id=mwA1k><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA1o><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA1s>[</span>130<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA1w>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt428 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-131 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-131"}}'><a href=#cite_note-131 id=mwA10><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA14><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA18>[</span>131<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA2A>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwA2E>From 2010 to 2012, <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexa_Internet title="Alexa Internet" id=mwA2I>Alexa</a> ranked YouTube as the third most visited website on the Internet after <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google title=Google id=mwA2M>Google</a> and Facebook.<sup about=#mwt431 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-132 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-132"}}'><a href=#cite_note-132 id=mwA2Q><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA2U><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA2Y>[</span>132<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA2c>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwA2g>In late 2011 and early 2012, YouTube launched over 100 <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/YouTube_Original_Channel_Initiative title="YouTube Original Channel Initiative" id=mwA2k>"premium" or "original"</a> channels. It was reported the initiative cost $100 million.<sup about=#mwt434 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-133 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-133"}}'><a href=#cite_note-133 id=mwA2o><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA2s><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA2w>[</span>133<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA20>]</span></span></a></sup> Two years later, in November 2013, it was documented that the landing page of the original channels became a 404 error page.<sup about=#mwt437 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-134 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-134"}}'><a href=#cite_note-134 id=mwA24><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA28><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA3A>[</span>134<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA3E>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt440 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-135 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-135"}}'><a href=#cite_note-135 id=mwA3I><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA3M><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA3Q>[</span>135<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA3U>]</span></span></a></sup> Despite this, original channels such as <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SourceFed title=SourceFed id=mwA3Y>SourceFed</a> and <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crash_Course_(web_series) title="Crash Course (web series)" id=mwA3c>Crash Course</a> were able to become successful.<sup about=#mwt443 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-136 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-136"}}'><a href=#cite_note-136 id=mwA3g><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA3k><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA3o>[</span>136<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA3s>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt446 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-137 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-137"}}'><a href=#cite_note-137 id=mwA3w><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA30><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA34>[</span>137<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA38>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwA4A>An algorithm change was made in 2012 that replaced the view-based system for a watch time-based one that is credited for causing a surge in the popularity of <a rel=mw:WikiLink href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Let's_Play" title="Let's Play" id=mwA4E>gaming channels</a>. It was meant to reduce the impact of channels releasing low effort videos for profit.<sup about=#mwt449 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-138 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-138"}}'><a href=#cite_note-138 id=mwA4I><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA4M><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA4Q>[</span>138<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA4U>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwA4Y>In October 2012, for the first-time ever, YouTube offered a live stream of the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_presidential_election_debates title="United States presidential election debates" class=mw-redirect id=mwA4c>U.S. presidential debate</a> and partnered with <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ABC_News_(United_States) title="ABC News (United States)" id=mwA4g>ABC News</a> to do so.<sup about=#mwt452 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-139 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-139"}}'><a href=#cite_note-139 id=mwA4k><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA4o><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA4s>[</span>139<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA4w>]</span></span></a></sup> The peak in concurrent views on any live stream was reached on October 14, where over eight million watched a <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Bull_Stratos title="Red Bull Stratos" id=mwA40>sky dive</a>.<sup about=#mwt455 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-140 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-140"}}'><a href=#cite_note-140 id=mwA44><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA48><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA5A>[</span>140<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA5E>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwA5I>In September 2012, with the release of <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IOS_6 title="IOS 6" id=mwA5M>iOS 6</a>, the app, which had been a default app on the OS since its release, was removed.<sup about=#mwt458 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-141 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-141"}}'><a href=#cite_note-141 id=mwA5Q><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA5U><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA5Y>[</span>141<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA5c>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt461 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-142 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-142"}}'><a href=#cite_note-142 id=mwA5g><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA5k><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA5o>[</span>142<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA5s>]</span></span></a></sup> Apple told <i id=mwA5w><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Verge title="The Verge" id=mwA50>The Verge</a></i> that the reason for the removal was due to an expired license, but that YouTube users could still view videos through the Safari web browser. The company also confirmed that <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google title=Google id=mwA54>Google</a>, which owns YouTube, was developing its own app, with a then-upcoming release through the App Store.<sup about=#mwt464 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-143 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-143"}}'><a href=#cite_note-143 id=mwA58><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA6A><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA6E>[</span>143<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA6I>]</span></span></a></sup> The Apple-developed YouTube app remained on iOS 5 and previous iOS versions.<sup about=#mwt467 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-144 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-144"}}'><a href=#cite_note-144 id=mwA6M><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA6Q><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA6U>[</span>144<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA6Y>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwA6c>On <span about=#mwt468 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwA6g data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Start date","href":"./Template:Start_date"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2012"},"2":{"wt":"10"},"3":{"wt":"25"}},"i":0}}]}'>October</span><span typeof=mw:Entity about=#mwt468>&nbsp;</span><span about=#mwt468>25,</span><span typeof=mw:Entity about=#mwt468>&nbsp;</span><span about=#mwt468>2012</span><span style=display:none about=#mwt468 id=mwA6k>()</span>, the YouTube slogan (Broadcast Yourself) was taken down due to the live stream of the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_presidential_election_debates title="United States presidential election debates" class=mw-redirect id=mwA6o>U.S. presidential debate</a>.</p>
<p id=mwA6s>In October 2012, YouTube introduced the ability to add a translucent and overlaid custom icon at a corner of all own videos, which can link to the channel page or a specified video. The feature was initially named "InVideo Programming".<sup about=#mwt471 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-145 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-145"}}'><a href=#cite_note-145 id=mwA6w><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA60><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA64>[</span>145<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA68>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwA7A>YouTube relaunched its design and layout in early December 2012 <span class=citation-needed-content style="padding-left:0.1em;padding-right:0.1em;color:var(--color-subtle,#54595d);border:1px solid var(--border-color-subtle,#c8ccd1)" about=#mwt472 typeof=mw:Transclusion id=mwA7E data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"citation needed span","href":"./Template:Citation_needed_span"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"to resemble the mobile and tablet app version of the site."},"date":{"wt":"September 2021"}},"i":0}}]}'>to resemble the mobile and tablet app version of the site.</span><link rel=mw:PageProp/Category href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:All_articles_with_unsourced_statements about=#mwt472><link rel=mw:PageProp/Category href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Articles_with_unsourced_statements_from_September_2021 about=#mwt472><sup class="noprint Inline-Template Template-Fact" style=margin-left:0.1em;white-space:nowrap about=#mwt472 id=mwA7I><span typeof=mw:Entity>[</span><i><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed title="Wikipedia:Citation needed"><span title="This claim needs references to reliable sources. (September 2021)">citation needed</span></a></i><span typeof=mw:Entity>]</span></sup> Notable changes of the watch page are the relocation of title and the "Subscribe" button from above to below the video's viewport, the removal of the button that opened a section above the video viewport showing other videos of the same channel without needing to leave the watch page, and the removal of a button-sized <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_banner title="Web banner" id=mwA7M>banner</a> located above the viewport, which could contain a custom image, popularly icons and text logos.<sup about=#mwt475 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-146 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-146"}}'><a href=#cite_note-146 id=mwA7Q><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA7U><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA7Y>[</span>146<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA7c>]</span></span></a></sup> Playlists on the watch page, which were formerly displayed as collapsible horizontal list fixed at the page bottom, became a scrollable vertical list next to the video player.<sup about=#mwt482 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-147 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-147"}}'><a href=#cite_note-147 id=mwA7g><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA7k><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA7o>[</span>147<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA7s>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwA7w>On December 21, 2012, the "<a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gangnam_Style title="Gangnam Style" id=mwA70>Gangnam Style</a>" music video by <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Korea title="South Korea" id=mwA74>South Korean</a> musician <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PSY title=PSY class=mw-redirect id=mwA78>PSY</a> became the first YouTube video to surpass one billion views.<sup about=#mwt485 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-148 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-148"}}'><a href=#cite_note-148 id=mwA8A><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA8E><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA8I>[</span>148<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA8M>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwA8Q>In 2012, Youtube launched a trusted flagger program to mass report videos for violating its guidelines.<sup about=#mwt488 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-149 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-149"}}'><a href=#cite_note-149 id=mwA8U><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA8Y><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA8c>[</span>149<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA8g>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt491 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-150 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-150"}}'><a href=#cite_note-150 id=mwA8k><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA8o><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA8s>[</span>150<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA8w>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt494 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-151 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-151"}}'><a href=#cite_note-151 id=mwA80><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA84><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA88>[</span>151<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA9A>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwA9E>As of early 2013, YouTube video recommendations contain both videos and channels.<sup about=#mwt495 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-152 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-152"}}'><a href=#cite_note-152 id=mwA9I><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA9M><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA9Q>[</span>152<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA9U>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
</section><section data-mw-section-id=4 id=mwA9Y aria-labelledby=Rise_of_YouTube_stars_and_feature_trim_down_(2013–2019)><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=Rise_of_YouTube_stars_and_feature_trim_down_(2013–2019)><span id=Rise_of_YouTube_stars_and_feature_trim_down_.282013.E2.80.932019.29 typeof=mw:FallbackId></span>Rise of YouTube stars and feature trim down (2013–2019)</h2></div>
<p id=mwA9c>In March 2013, YouTube introduced a new layout for channels known as "One Channel", which added the ability to put playlists into shelves on the channel front page, but removed custom backgrounds. Formerly unified channel pages were separated into multiple sub pages such as "Videos", "Playlists", "Discussion" (channel comments), "Channels" (featured by user), and "About" (channel description, total video view count, join date, outlinks). This layout was initially optional, with a transitional period taking place between March 8 and June 5 after which it has been made permanent for all users. This layout formed the basis of the one currently used going forward.<sup about=#mwt498 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-153 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-153"}}'><a href=#cite_note-153 id=mwA9g><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA9k><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA9o>[</span>153<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA9s>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwA9w>In March 2013, the number of unique users visiting YouTube every month reached 1 billion.<sup about=#mwt501 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-154 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-154"}}'><a href=#cite_note-154 id=mwA90><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA94><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA98>[</span>154<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA-A>]</span></span></a></sup> In the same year, YouTube continued to reach out to mainstream media, launching <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/YouTube_Comedy_Week title="YouTube Comedy Week" id=mwA-E>YouTube Comedy Week</a> and the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/YouTube_Music_Awards title="YouTube Music Awards" id=mwA-I>YouTube Music Awards</a>.<sup about=#mwt504 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-155 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-155"}}'><a href=#cite_note-155 id=mwA-M><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA-Q><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA-U>[</span>155<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA-Y>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt507 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-156 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-156"}}'><a href=#cite_note-156 id=mwA-c><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA-g><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA-k>[</span>156<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA-o>]</span></span></a></sup> Both events were met with negative to mixed reception.<sup about=#mwt510 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-157 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-157"}}'><a href=#cite_note-157 id=mwA-s><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA-w><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA-0>[</span>157<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA-4>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt513 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-158 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-158"}}'><a href=#cite_note-158 id=mwA-8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA_A><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA_E>[</span>158<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA_I>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt516 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-159 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-159"}}'><a href=#cite_note-159 id=mwA_M><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA_Q><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA_U>[</span>159<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA_Y>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt519 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-160 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-160"}}'><a href=#cite_note-160 id=mwA_c><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA_g><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA_k>[</span>160<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA_o>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwA_s>Automatically generated playlists known as "YouTube Mix" were first rolled out in April 2013.<sup about=#mwt522 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-161 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-161"}}'><a href=#cite_note-161 id=mwA_w><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwA_0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwA_4>[</span>161<span class=cite-bracket id=mwA_8>]</span></span></a></sup> A year later, the feature was rolled out to the mobile app for Android OS.<sup about=#mwt525 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-NDTV-2014-04_162-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"NDTV-2014-04"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-NDTV-2014-04-162"}}'><a href=#cite_note-NDTV-2014-04-162 id=mwBAA><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBAE><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBAI>[</span>162<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBAM>]</span></span></a></sup> A similar feature called "YouTube Radio" for continuous music playback in resemblance to radio stations was tested in February 2015.<sup about=#mwt528 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-163 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-163"}}'><a href=#cite_note-163 id=mwBAQ><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBAU><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBAY>[</span>163<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBAc>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBAg>Since approximately July 9, 2013, the first page of videos' comment section is no longer included in the watch page's static <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTML title=HTML id=mwBAk>HTML</a> source code, but instead loaded subsequently through <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajax_(programming) title="Ajax (programming)" id=mwBAo>AJAX</a>.<sup about=#mwt533 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-164 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-164"}}'><a href=#cite_note-164 id=mwBAs><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBAw><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBA0>[</span>164<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBA4>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBA8>A <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Picture-in-picture title=Picture-in-picture id=mwBBA>picture-in-picture</a> mode for browsing within the app while watching was introduced to the mobile app in August 2013.<sup about=#mwt536 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-165 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-165"}}'><a href=#cite_note-165 id=mwBBE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBBI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBBM>[</span>165<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBBQ>]</span></span></a></sup> At a similar time, channel hover cards were first implemented to the desktop site, which are <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tooltip title=Tooltip id=mwBBU>tooltips</a> previewing channel details that appear when <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mouseover title=Mouseover id=mwBBY>pointing</a> at channel names with the mouse cursor. These details include the header image, subscriber count, subscribe button, and a snippet of the channel description text.<sup about=#mwt539 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-166 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-166"}}'><a href=#cite_note-166 id=mwBBc><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBBg><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBBk>[</span>166<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBBo>]</span></span></a></sup> Additionally, a <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Media_control_symbols#Symbols title="Media control symbols" id=mwBBs>play symbol</a> ("▶") to indicate a playing video in the page title was added to the desktop site. But it has been rendered obsolete the following years as desktop <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_browser title="Web browser" id=mwBBw>web browsers</a> were equipped with an indicator for audio-playing tabs.<sup about=#mwt542 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-167 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-167"}}'><a href=#cite_note-167 id=mwBB0><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBB4><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBB8>[</span>167<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBCA>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt545 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-168 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-168"}}'><a href=#cite_note-168 id=mwBCE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBCI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBCM>[</span>168<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBCQ>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt548 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-169 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-169"}}'><a href=#cite_note-169 id=mwBCU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBCY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBCc>[</span>169<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBCg>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBCk>On September 12, 2013, the "video responses" feature introduced back in May 2006 was discontinued, citing a low <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Click-through_rate title="Click-through rate" id=mwBCo>click-through rate</a>. It allowed users to respond to videos through a new or existing video which appeared above the comment section.<sup about=#mwt551 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-170 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-170"}}'><a href=#cite_note-170 id=mwBCs><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBCw><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBC0>[</span>170<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBC4>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBC8>From September to November 2013, YouTube's comment system on channel pages and videos, were integrated to Google's <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network title="Social network" id=mwBDA>social network</a> site "<a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google+ title=Google+ id=mwBDE>Google+</a>", since which a Google Brand Account is required to be able to comment. This change also included the ability to edit existing comments and include <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/URL title=URL id=mwBDI>URLs</a> in comments, with the removal of the 500 characters limit and negative user ratings from comments. Channels created prior as standalone YouTube accounts using its legacy registration form have been <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grandfather_clause title="Grandfather clause" id=mwBDM>grandfathered</a> to a <code id=mwBDQ>/user/</code> URL.<sup about=#mwt554 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-171 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-171"}}'><a href=#cite_note-171 id=mwBDU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBDY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBDc>[</span>171<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBDg>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt557 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-172 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-172"}}'><a href=#cite_note-172 id=mwBDk><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBDo><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBDs>[</span>172<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBDw>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBD0>In November 2013, YouTube's own YouTube channel surpassed Felix Kjellberg's <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PewDiePie title=PewDiePie id=mwBD4>PewDiePie</a> channel to become the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_most-subscribed_YouTube_channels title="List of most-subscribed YouTube channels" id=mwBD8>most subscribed channel</a> on the website. This was due to auto-suggesting new users to subscribe to the channel upon registration.<sup about=#mwt560 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-173 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-173"}}'><a href=#cite_note-173 id=mwBEA><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBEE><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBEI>[</span>173<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBEM>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBEQ>Users of the mobile app gained the ability to reply to comments in April 2014.<sup about=#mwt561 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-NDTV-2014-04_162-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"NDTV-2014-04"}}'><a href=#cite_note-NDTV-2014-04-162 id=mwBEU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBEY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBEc>[</span>162<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBEg>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBEk>In June 2014, YouTube replaced the classic Inbox feature with a new private messaging system, which – like comments – required users to have their YouTube accounts linked with a Google+ profile, which was subsequently moved over to Google Brand Accounts. Legacy Inbox messages could be viewed and downloaded up until December 1.<sup about=#mwt564 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-174 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-174"}}'><a href=#cite_note-174 id=mwBEo><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBEs><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBEw>[</span>174<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBE0>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt567 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-175 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-175"}}'><a href=#cite_note-175 id=mwBE4><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBE8><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBFA>[</span>175<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBFE>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBFI>In October 2014, videos' frame rate limit was increased from 30 to 60, allowing for a smoother and more realistic appearance. It was initially only available with Google Chrome and later expanded to other browsers. 60fps are only available at 720p resolution and above.<sup about=#mwt570 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-176 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-176"}}'><a href=#cite_note-176 id=mwBFM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBFQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBFU>[</span>176<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBFY>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBFc>In November 2014, YouTube launched a paid <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Subscription_service title="Subscription service" class=mw-redirect id=mwBFg>subscription service</a> initially named "Music Key", featuring background playback, the integrated ability to download music for offline use, and no advertisement breaks.<sup about=#mwt573 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-177 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-177"}}'><a href=#cite_note-177 id=mwBFk><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBFo><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBFs>[</span>177<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBFw>]</span></span></a></sup> Almost a year later, in October 2015, it was rebranded to "YouTube Red" and its scope expanded beyond music.<sup about=#mwt576 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-178 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-178"}}'><a href=#cite_note-178 id=mwBF0><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBF4><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBF8>[</span>178<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBGA>]</span></span></a></sup> It was rebranded again in May 2018 to "YouTube Premium", and its availability expanded across countries.<sup about=#mwt579 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-179 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-179"}}'><a href=#cite_note-179 id=mwBGE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBGI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBGM>[</span>179<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBGQ>]</span></span></a></sup> Google's other music streaming service <i id=mwBGU><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google_Play_Music title="Google Play Music" id=mwBGY>Play Music</a></i> was merged with <i id=mwBGc>YouTube Music</i> in May 2020, as the latter is a more recognized brand.<sup about=#mwt582 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-180 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-180"}}'><a href=#cite_note-180 id=mwBGg><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBGk><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBGo>[</span>180<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBGs>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBGw>Support for the dedicated YouTube application on the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PlayStation_Vita title="PlayStation Vita" id=mwBG0>Sony PlayStation Vita</a> game console was deprecated in January 2015, for the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_Wii title="Nintendo Wii" class=mw-redirect id=mwBG4>Nintendo Wii</a> and Wii Mini in June 2017, and for the <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_3DS title="Nintendo 3DS" id=mwBG8>Nintendo 3DS</a> in August 2019.<sup about=#mwt585 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-181 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-181"}}'><a href=#cite_note-181 id=mwBHA><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBHE><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBHI>[</span>181<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBHM>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt588 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-182 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-182"}}'><a href=#cite_note-182 id=mwBHQ><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBHU><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBHY>[</span>182<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBHc>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt591 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-183 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-183"}}'><a href=#cite_note-183 id=mwBHg><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBHk><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBHo>[</span>183<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBHs>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBHw>In March 2015, YouTube introduced the ability to automatically publish videos at a scheduled time,<sup about=#mwt594 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-184 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-184"}}'><a href=#cite_note-184 id=mwBH0><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBH4><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBH8>[</span>184<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBIA>]</span></span></a></sup> as well as "info cards" and "end cards", which allow referring to videos and channels through a notification at the top right of the video at any playback time, and thumbnails shown in the last 20 seconds. In contrary to annotations, these work in the mobile app too, though are far less customizable.<sup about=#mwt597 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-185 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-185"}}'><a href=#cite_note-185 id=mwBIE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBII><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBIM>[</span>185<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBIQ>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt600 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-186 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-186"}}'><a href=#cite_note-186 id=mwBIU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBIY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBIc>[</span>186<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBIg>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBIk><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/360-degree_video title="360-degree video" id=mwBIo>360-degree video</a> was launched in March 2015. A year later, in April 2016, the ability to live stream 360-degree video was launched. Additionally, live streaming resolution was elevated to 1440p and 60 frames per second, and support for the <i id=mwBIs><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EIA-608 title=EIA-608 id=mwBIw>EIA-608</a></i> and <i id=mwBI0><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CEA-708 title=CEA-708 class=mw-redirect id=mwBI4>CEA-708</a></i> formats were added for embedded captioning.<sup about=#mwt603 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-187 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-187"}}'><a href=#cite_note-187 id=mwBI8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBJA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBJE>[</span>187<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBJI>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBJM>In August 2015, "YouTube Gaming" was launched. It was a separate web and mobile front end showing only gaming-related content, featuring a similar layout but somewhat modified appearance compared to the main site, and a <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light-on-dark_color_scheme title="Light-on-dark color scheme" class=mw-redirect id=mwBJQ>light-on-dark color scheme</a> well before the feature was <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=#darkTheme2017 class=mw-selflink-fragment id=mwBJU>introduced to the main site</a>.<sup about=#mwt606 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-188 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-188"}}'><a href=#cite_note-188 id=mwBJY><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBJc><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBJg>[</span>188<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBJk>]</span></span></a></sup> It was discontinued in March 2019 and merged with the main site.<sup about=#mwt609 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-189 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-189"}}'><a href=#cite_note-189 id=mwBJo><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBJs><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBJw>[</span>189<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBJ0>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBJ4>At a similar time, the view count indicator was patched to become continuous instead of temporary halting at 301 views (indicated as "301+") for hours, reportedly to calculate and deduct "counterfeit views". This phenomenon was first documented in June 2012. As an <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Easter_egg_(media) title="Easter egg (media)" id=mwBJ8>easter egg</a>, the view counter of the video by mathematics channel "<a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Numberphile title=Numberphile id=mwBKA>Numberphile</a>" discussing this phenomenon was set to remain at 301.<sup about=#mwt612 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-190 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-190"}}'><a href=#cite_note-190 id=mwBKE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBKI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBKM>[</span>190<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBKQ>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt615 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-191 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-191"}}'><a href=#cite_note-191 id=mwBKU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBKY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBKc>[</span>191<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBKg>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt619 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-192 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-192"}}'><a href=#cite_note-192 id=mwBKk><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBKo><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBKs>[</span>192<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBKw>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBK0>In December 2015 and January 2016, direct uploading through email and webcam recording respectively were removed. The former existed to support cell phones with limited web browsing capabilities.<sup about=#mwt622 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-193 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-193"}}'><a href=#cite_note-193 id=mwBK4><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBK8><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBLA>[</span>193<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBLE>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt625 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-194 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-194"}}'><a href=#cite_note-194 id=mwBLI><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBLM><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBLQ>[</span>194<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBLU>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBLY>Around January 30, 2016, the dedicated "<code id=mwBLc>/all_comments</code>" page which served videos' comments as static HTML was removed as well, and redirected to videos' main watch page, "<code id=mwBLg>/watch</code>".<sup about=#mwt632 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-195 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-195"}}'><a href=#cite_note-195 id=mwBLk><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBLo><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBLs>[</span>195<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBLw>]</span></span></a></sup> At some point, "<code id=mwBL0>/all_comments</code>" displayed the absolute date (e. g. "Aug 26, 2014") rather than the relative (e. g. "1 week ago") on older comments,<sup about=#mwt635 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-196 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-196"}}'><a href=#cite_note-196 id=mwBL4><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBL8><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBMA>[</span>196<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBME>]</span></span></a></sup> as well as 500 comments per page like on legacy Reddit and three preview thumbnails from a video.<sup about=#mwt638 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-all_comments_2012_83-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"all_comments_2012"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-all_comments_2012-83"}}'><a href=#cite_note-all_comments_2012-83 id=mwBMI><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBMM><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBMQ>[</span>83<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBMU>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBMY><link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1349394836 about=#mwt639 typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id=mwBMc data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Template:Visible anchor/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Visible anchor","href":"./Template:Visible_anchor"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Polymer2016"},"text":{"wt":"In mid-2016, the earliest experiments with a redesigned desktop web front end were conducted."}},"i":0}}]}'><span class=vanchor about=#mwt639 id=mwBMg><span id=Polymer2016></span><span class=vanchor-text>In mid-2016, the earliest experiments with a redesigned desktop web front end were conducted.</span></span> It follows the "<a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Material_design title="Material design" class=mw-redirect id=mwBMk>material design</a>" language and is based on the "Polymer" <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_framework title="Web framework" id=mwBMo>web framework</a>.<sup about=#mwt645 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-material2016_197-0 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"material2016"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-material2016-197"}}'><a href=#cite_note-material2016-197 id=mwBMs><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBMw><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBM0>[</span>197<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBM4>]</span></span></a></sup> <link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1349394836 about=#mwt641 typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id=mwBM8 data-mw="{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;templatestyles&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;Template:Visible anchor/styles.css&quot;},&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;template&quot;:{&quot;target&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;visible anchor&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:&quot;./Template:Visible_anchor&quot;},&quot;params&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;darkTheme2017&quot;},&quot;text&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;A [[light-on-dark color scheme]], also known as \&quot;dark mode\&quot; or \&quot;dark theme\&quot;, was first implemented in May 2017, though it wouldn't release until August 2017.&quot;}},&quot;i&quot;:0}}]}"><span class=vanchor about=#mwt641 id=mwBNA><span id=darkTheme2017></span><span class=vanchor-text>A <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light-on-dark_color_scheme title="Light-on-dark color scheme" class=mw-redirect>light-on-dark color scheme</a>, also known as "dark mode" or "dark theme", was first implemented in May 2017, though it wouldn't release until August 2017.</span></span><sup about=#mwt648 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-198 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-198"}}'><a href=#cite_note-198 id=mwBNE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBNI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBNM>[</span>198<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBNQ>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBNU>The earliest trials with a new channel sub page named <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=#communitypage class=mw-selflink-fragment id=mwBNY>"Community"</a> as an impending replacement for "Discussion" were conducted on select channels in September 2016.<sup about=#mwt651 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-199 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-199"}}'><a href=#cite_note-199 id=mwBNc><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBNg><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBNk>[</span>199<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBNo>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBNs>In November 2016, the ability to "heart" and pin comments under own videos was added. "Hearting" visibly marks comments under own videos to signify appreciation; a select comment can be pinned so to remain on top of the section.<sup about=#mwt654 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-200 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-200"}}'><a href=#cite_note-200 id=mwBNw><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBN0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBN4>[</span>200<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBN8>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBOA>Since December 2016, YouTube started rolling out a progress bar at thumbnails' bottom edge, indicating the watch progress of previously watched videos, starting with the iOS app.<sup about=#mwt657 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-201 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-201"}}'><a href=#cite_note-201 id=mwBOE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBOI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBOM>[</span>201<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBOQ>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBOU>Live streaming from the mobile app was rolled out in early 2017, initially only available to channels with at least 10,000 subscribers.<sup about=#mwt660 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-202 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-202"}}'><a href=#cite_note-202 id=mwBOY><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBOc><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBOg>[</span>202<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBOk>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBOo><link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1349394836 about=#mwt661 typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id=mwBOs data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Template:Visible anchor/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Visible anchor","href":"./Template:Visible_anchor"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Annotations_locked"},"text":{"wt":"[[#Annotations|Annotations]] became uneditable on May 2, 2017."}},"i":0}}]}'><span class=vanchor about=#mwt661 id=mwBOw><span id=Annotations_locked></span><span class=vanchor-text><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=#Annotations class=mw-selflink-fragment>Annotations</a> became uneditable on May 2, 2017.</span></span> Since then, users were only able to remove all annotations from individual videos. Parts of the feature such as pause markings were already removed earlier, with collaborative annotations having been discontinued in December 2010.<sup about=#mwt665 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-203 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-203"}}'><a href=#cite_note-203 id=mwBO0><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBO4><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBO8>[</span>203<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBPA>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt668 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-204 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-204"}}'><a href=#cite_note-204 id=mwBPE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBPI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBPM>[</span>204<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBPQ>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt671 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-205 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-205"}}'><a href=#cite_note-205 id=mwBPU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBPY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBPc>[</span>205<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBPg>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBPk><link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1349394836 about=#mwt672 typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id=mwBPo data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Template:Visible anchor/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Visible anchor","href":"./Template:Visible_anchor"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Redesign2017"},"text":{"wt":"On August 29, 2017, YouTube changed both their logo and the design of their desktop website."}},"i":0}}]}'><span class=vanchor about=#mwt672 id=mwBPs><span id=Redesign2017></span><span class=vanchor-text>On August 29, 2017, YouTube changed both their logo and the design of their desktop website.</span></span> The "Tube" part of the logo is no longer surrounded by the shape resembling a <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CRT_television title="CRT television" class=mw-redirect id=mwBPw>CRT television</a>. The shape moved left besides the "YouTube" word mark and has a white triangle resembling a <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Play_button title="Play button" class=mw-redirect id=mwBP0>play button</a>. Their new "Polymer" web front based on that <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=#Polymer2016 class=mw-selflink-fragment id=mwBP4>first tested in mid-2016</a> was made default for visitors.<sup about=#mwt676 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-206 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-206"}}'><a href=#cite_note-206 id=mwBP8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBQA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBQE>[</span>206<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBQI>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt677 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-material2016_197-1 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"material2016"}}'><a href=#cite_note-material2016-197 id=mwBQM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBQQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBQU>[</span>197<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBQY>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBQc>As of 2017, notes could be added to videos within playlists by the creator of the playlist.<sup about=#mwt680 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-207 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-207"}}'><a href=#cite_note-207 id=mwBQg><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBQk><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBQo>[</span>207<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBQs>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBQw>In January 2018, a <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Musical_note title="Musical note" id=mwBQ0>musical note</a> badge replaced the check mark to denote <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Account_verification title="Account verification" id=mwBQ4>account verification</a> status for music artists. YouTube refers to such channels as "artist channels", a feature introduced months prior with a slightly different channel layout.<sup about=#mwt683 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-208 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-208"}}'><a href=#cite_note-208 id=mwBQ8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBRA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBRE>[</span>208<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBRI>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBRM><link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1349394836 about=#mwt684 typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id=mwBRQ data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Template:Visible anchor/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Visible anchor","href":"./Template:Visible_anchor"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"PIP"},"text":{"wt":"In March 2018, a [[picture-in-picture]] mode was introduced to the desktop web site"}},"i":0}}]}'><span class=vanchor about=#mwt684 id=mwBRU><span id=PIP></span><span class=vanchor-text>In March 2018, a <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Picture-in-picture title=Picture-in-picture>picture-in-picture</a> mode was introduced to the desktop web site</span></span> that the fixes the video player to the lower right corner of the screen for browsing and searching without having to leave the video. A fixed "mini player" top bar appearing when scrolling down and containing the video and controls for watching while browsing comments was intermittently tested.<sup about=#mwt688 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-209 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-209"}}'><a href=#cite_note-209 id=mwBRY><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBRc><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBRg>[</span>209<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBRk>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt691 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-210 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-210"}}'><a href=#cite_note-210 id=mwBRo><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBRs><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBRw>[</span>210<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBR0>]</span></span></a></sup> At a similar time, YouTube consolidated music artists' <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vevo title=Vevo id=mwBR4>Vevo</a> channels with their original channels, merging their subscriber base.<sup about=#mwt694 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-211 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-211"}}'><a href=#cite_note-211 id=mwBR8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBSA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBSE>[</span>211<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBSI>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBSM>On April 3, 2018, <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/YouTube_headquarters_shooting title="YouTube headquarters shooting" class=mw-redirect id=mwBSQ>a shooting</a> took place at YouTube headquarters.<sup about=#mwt697 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-212 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-212"}}'><a href=#cite_note-212 id=mwBSU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBSY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBSc>[</span>212<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBSg>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBSk><link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1349394836 about=#mwt698 typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id=mwBSo data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Template:Visible anchor/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Visible anchor","href":"./Template:Visible_anchor"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Premiere"},"text":{"wt":"In June 2018, a \"Premiere\" feature was added"}},"i":0}}]}'><span class=vanchor about=#mwt698 id=mwBSs><span id=Premiere></span><span class=vanchor-text>In June 2018, a "Premiere" feature was added</span></span>, where a video can be broadcast like a live stream after uploaded, and users can discuss in a live chat like they can in live streams. Before the video starts, an animated two-minute preroll with the soundtrack "Space Walk" by "Silent Partner" is played. A premiere can be set to start immediately after upload or at a scheduled time, though scheduled publications existed since March 2015.<sup about=#mwt702 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-213 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-213"}}'><a href=#cite_note-213 id=mwBSw><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBS0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBS4>[</span>213<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBS8>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt705 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-214 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-214"}}'><a href=#cite_note-214 id=mwBTA><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBTE><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBTI>[</span>214<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBTM>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBTQ>On July 9, 2018, the private messaging feature has been removed from "Creator Studio", purging existing messages.<sup about=#mwt708 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-215 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-215"}}'><a href=#cite_note-215 id=mwBTU><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBTY><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBTc>[</span>215<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBTg>]</span></span></a></sup></p> 
<p id=mwBTk>In July 2018, it was reported that the site's "Polymer" redesign slowed performance significantly on non-<a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chromium_(web_browser) title="Chromium (web browser)" id=mwBTo>Chromium</a> browsers compared to the legacy, <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Progressive_enhancement title="Progressive enhancement" id=mwBTs>HTML-based</a> version of the front end.<sup about=#mwt711 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-216 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-216"}}'><a href=#cite_note-216 id=mwBTw><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBT0><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBT4>[</span>216<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBT8>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt714 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-217 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-217"}}'><a href=#cite_note-217 id=mwBUA><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBUE><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBUI>[</span>217<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBUM>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt717 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-218 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-218"}}'><a href=#cite_note-218 id=mwBUQ><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBUU><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBUY>[</span>218<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBUc>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBUg>In August 2018, the search result counter resembling that of <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google_Search#Page_layout title="Google Search" id=mwBUk>Google Search</a> was removed.<sup about=#mwt724 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-219 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-219"}}'><a href=#cite_note-219 id=mwBUo><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBUs><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBUw>[</span>219<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBU0>]</span></span></a></sup> The change occurred one month after the airing of a popular <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TED_talk title="TED talk" class=mw-redirect id=mwBU4>TED talk</a> with a prominent mention of a result count of 10 million for a search for <i id=mwBU8><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinder_Surprise title="Kinder Surprise" id=mwBVA>surprise eggs</a></i>.<sup about=#mwt727 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-220 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-220"}}'><a href=#cite_note-220 id=mwBVE><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBVI><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBVM>[</span>220<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBVQ>]</span></span></a></sup> Whether it is related is unknown.</p>
<p id=mwBVU>In October 2018, YouTube announced launching a fund program for educational creators, to which creators with a minimum of 25.000 subscribers and a demonstrated expertise in their field could apply through an agreement.<sup about=#mwt730 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-221 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-221"}}'><a href=#cite_note-221 id=mwBVY><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBVc><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBVg>[</span>221<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBVk>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBVo><link rel=mw-deduplicated-inline-style href=mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1349394836 about=#mwt731 typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id=mwBVs data-mw="{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;templatestyles&quot;,&quot;attrs&quot;:{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;Template:Visible anchor/styles.css&quot;},&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;template&quot;:{&quot;target&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;Visible anchor&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:&quot;./Template:Visible_anchor&quot;},&quot;params&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;Stories&quot;},&quot;text&quot;:{&quot;wt&quot;:&quot;In November 2018, YouTube rolled out a [[YouTube#YouTube Stories|\&quot;Stories\&quot;]] feature in resemblance to ''[[Snapchat]]'' and ''[[Instagram Stories]]''&quot;}},&quot;i&quot;:0}}]}"><span class=vanchor about=#mwt731 id=mwBVw><span id=Stories></span><span class=vanchor-text>In November 2018, YouTube rolled out a <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/YouTube#YouTube_Stories title=YouTube>"Stories"</a> feature in resemblance to <i><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snapchat title=Snapchat>Snapchat</a></i> and <i><a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instagram_Stories title="Instagram Stories" class=mw-redirect>Instagram Stories</a></i></span></span>, where videos are automatically deleted ("expire") after a day. The feature was tested as "YouTube reels" earlier that year, and is only accessible through the native <a rel=mw:WikiLink href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mobile_app title="Mobile app" id=mwBV0>mobile apps</a> and not implemented on the websites.<sup about=#mwt735 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-222 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-222"}}'><a href=#cite_note-222 id=mwBV4><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBV8><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBWA>[</span>222<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBWE>]</span></span></a></sup> This feature was disabled on June 26, 2023, citing access being limited, and poor promotion. Existing stories would remain for 7 days after uploading was disabled.<sup about=#mwt738 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-223 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-223"}}'><a href=#cite_note-223 id=mwBWI><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBWM><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBWQ>[</span>223<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBWU>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt741 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-224 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-224"}}'><a href=#cite_note-224 id=mwBWY><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBWc><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBWg>[</span>224<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBWk>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<p id=mwBWo>Throughout 2018, YouTube rolled out the ability to save videos to a mobile device for a limited period for offline viewing. Videos are saved in a way they are only accessible from inside the app, not from external software.<sup about=#mwt744 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-225 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-225"}}'><a href=#cite_note-225 id=mwBWs><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBWw><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBW0>[</span>225<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBW4>]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=#mwt747 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-226 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-226"}}'><a href=#cite_note-226 id=mwBW8><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBXA><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBXE>[</span>226<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBXI>]</span></span></a></sup> The feature was later made exclusive for members of the YouTube Premium subscription service.<sup about=#mwt750 class="mw-ref reference" id=cite_ref-227 rel=dc:references typeof=mw:Extension/ref data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-227"}}'><a href=#cite_note-227 id=mwBXM><span class=mw-reflink-text id=mwBXQ><span class=cite-bracket id=mwBXU>[</span>227<span class=cite-bracket id=mwBXY>]</span></span></a></sup></p>
</section><section data-mw-section-id=5 id=mwBXc aria-labelledby=Modern_era,_continued_feature_trim_down_and_refinement_(2019–present)><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=Modern_era,_continued_feature_trim_down_and_refinement_(2019–present)><span id=Modern_era.2C_continued_feature_trim_down_and_refinement_.282019.E2.80.93present.29 typeof=mw:FallbackId></span>Modern era, continued feature trim down and refinement (2019–present)</h2></div>
